@@ -108,6 +108,7 @@ Every `test_*.py` file is listed below, with the reason the module exists rather
 | [`test_model_protocol_menu.py`](test_model_protocol_menu.py) | `profiles_payload` already served `protocols: list(PROFILE_PROTOCOLS)` and the client ignored it, hardcoding the dropdown — so `gemini` and `openai_responses`, both dispatchable and both accepted by POST/PATCH, were unreachable from the UI. |
 | [`test_session_and_message_paging.py`](test_session_and_message_paging.py) | The session-list keyset cursor was real server-side and had **no production consumer anywhere in the repo**: `loadSessions` was hard-capped at `limit=100`, so a project with more than 100 sessions simply could not reach the rest. Walked over 260 sessions and 640 active-branch messages asserting no gap, no duplicate, and an export carrying the first and last message. The load-more control firing and the scroll anchor holding are browser-only and are named as such rather than faked with a grep against `app.js`. |
 | [`test_project_paging.py`](test_project_paging.py) | Home project directory paging. A 1000-row fixture still answers in pages of 100 (never more than 100); the `(last_active_at, project_id)` keyset covers a same-timestamp tie and empty `last_active_at` sorts last; a page is at most two `SELECT`s (JOIN aggregate + exact filtered total); escaped `LIKE` treats `\\`/`%`/`_` as literals on name/description, ASCII case-insensitive and non-ASCII exact; `q` over 128 code points is 400; a filter or principal change, or a malformed cursor, returns `400 invalid_cursor` rather than silently restarting at page one; team visibility is applied before `LIMIT` so hidden rows cannot occupy slots; unparameterized GET stays a full dump and `offset` is executed in the compatibility window. |
+| [`test_project_patch_unknown_id.py`](test_project_patch_unknown_id.py) | Whether editing a project that does not exist says so. `PUT`/`PATCH /projects/{id}` ran an `UPDATE` that matched no row — not an error — and answered `_project_json({})`, which is `{}`, so a deleted or mistyped id got 200 `{}` while a body naming no field on a real project answers the full project. The workbench's project modal took that 200 as a saved edit and closed. Both verbs are now `404 project not found` with nothing written, and the second test holds single-user "missing", team-mode "missing" and "not yours" from the project guard, and an admin's "missing" from the handler to one body (INV-13): the wording alone must not say which path refused. `GET /projects/{id}` deliberately keeps its documented `{}`. Both verbs cover refusal parity. Ordinary requests use the real handler and socket without a marker for schema capture; marked race tests delete the project before and after the real update and require 404. |
 | [`test_annotation_version_binding.py`](test_annotation_version_binding.py) | An image attachment's bytes were not bound to an immutable version: annotations recorded no version_id/checksum, and `resolve_artifact_path` returns the artifact's **latest** version — so overwriting the figure between pinning and sending silently changed what the model received. MIME is now decided by magic bytes, not the filename. |
 | [`test_artifact_refs.py`](test_artifact_refs.py) | What `@file` in a message actually sends. The old resolver read the artifact's *live path*, so the same reference meant different bytes once a later cell overwrote the file; an unresolvable name was dropped in silence, leaving the user asking about a file the model never received; and every artifact was decoded as UTF-8 with `errors="replace"`, so a `.npz` arrived as a wall of U+FFFD that reads as corrupted text rather than as "this is not text". Covers the pinned form, the one-release legacy spelling, cross-session materialisation at send, and the budgets. Also covers the budget the other two did not add up to: `MAX_REFS` bounds how many and `MAX_REF_BYTES` bounds each, but eight at the per-file cap is 1,600,000 characters — about 1.5× a 262,144-token window, and 8× the cap on the message a person types. References now share one budget, and what it cuts is named rather than dropped. |
 | [`test_artifact_index.py`](test_artifact_index.py) | The project Artifact index route. A 500-row fixture still answers in pages of 50 (never more than 100); the `(created_at, artifact_id)` keyset covers a same-millisecond tie; escaped `LIKE` treats `%`/`_` as literals and searches filename only; a filter change returns `400 invalid_cursor`; team visibility is applied before `LIMIT`; same-name artifacts across sessions/versions stay distinct; the legacy array route stays a bare array. The browse index is additive and rolls back with a failed migration step. |
